\ Reaching into a symbolic row with concrete-arity ops. A runtime-depth
\ `drop-at` removes an element at a runtime-computed depth and collapses the
\ remaining stack into an opaque row the AOT compiler cannot statically size.
\ Concrete-arity operations that then reach into that row -- `nip`, `swap drop`,
\ `over`, a literal-depth `pick-n`, and `#nth!` -- are emitted against the live
\ physical stack and collapse to a fresh row, instead of rejecting as an
\ abstract underflow. Built under runtime-image AOT, so these results come from
\ the compiled path and match the interpreter.

\ nip into the row: drop-at leaves [10 20 30 40]; nip drops 30 -> [10 20 40]
10 20 30 40 50 0 drop-at nip . . .
"--" print-line

\ swap drop into the row: leaves [10 20 30 40]; swap -> [10 20 40 30]; drop -> [10 20 40]
10 20 30 40 50 0 drop-at swap drop . . .
"--" print-line

\ over into the row: leaves [10 20 30 40]; over clones 30 -> [10 20 30 40 30]
10 20 30 40 50 0 drop-at over . . . . .
"--" print-line

\ literal-depth pick-n reaching into the row: leaves [10 20 30 40], push v=99;
\ `1 pick-n` clones the element one below v, which lives inside the row (40)
10 20 30 40 50 0 drop-at 99 1 pick-n . . . . . .
"--" print-line

\ #nth! reaching into the row for its seq and index: [V 0] collapses to a row,
\ then `99 #nth!` mutates V[0] through the row; read it back as 99
V{ 7 8 9 } 0 5 0 drop-at 99 #nth! 0 #nth .
